  That makes the substrate question tractable again, and it's still the one thing I can't design around.

  [1mThe fork:[0m where task data lives. This one is genuinely expensive to reverse — it decides whether you ever need auth,
  and whether "a task" is a thing on your machine or a thing on a server.

  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, browser-only[0m — zero infra, works offline, open the HTML file and it runs. Tasks are trapped in one
    browser on one device; no sharing, no sync, and clearing site data destroys everything. Rules out the multi-user
    notifications you originally asked for.
  - [1mBackend + DB[0m — tasks are real, shared, multi-device, and the original notifications request becomes buildable. Costs
    auth, a server, a deploy story, and turns an afternoon into a project.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m behind a storage interface[0m — same as the first, but reads/writes go through a small adapter so a
    server-backed one can replace it. The cost is one indirection layer; the risk is that it's speculative and the real
    backend rarely matches the interface you guessed.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, browser-only, no storage abstraction.[0m The app is one empty page — the fastest way to
  learn what you actually want from it is to have a working single-user version this week. I'd skip the adapter: the
  change-event chokepoint is worth building speculatively because it's ~10 lines and notifications definitely need it,
  whereas a storage interface is a bet on a specific backend shape you haven't chosen. If a server arrives later,
  swapping a small, well-bounded persistence module is a contained rewrite, not a rescue.
